{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Braem2005-LHCb-RICH2-Al-SiO2-HfO2",
  "Source": "A. Braem, C. David, and C. Joram, \"Metal multi-dielectric mirror coatings for Cherenkov detectors,\" Nucl. Instrum. Methods Phys. Res. A 553, 182-186 (2005). doi:10.1016/j.nima.2005.08.074 - Table 1 p. 185 (geometric thicknesses Cr 20, Al 85, SiO2 28, HfO2 38 nm); Sec. 5 p. 184 (stack Al + 1 pair SiO2/HfO2, HfO2 terminates the stack); Fig. 3",
  "Notes": "UV-enhanced aluminium mirror for the LHCb RICH2 spherical and flat mirrors: Al + 1 pair SiO2/HfO2, optimized for maximum reflectivity at 275 nm (HPD photocathode sensitivity)\nSpecification: Sec. 1 p. 183: R > 94 % for 250 < lambda < 300 nm and R > 80 % for 210 < lambda < 500 nm, at incidence angles 21 deg (spherical mirror) and 48 deg (flat mirror); coating optimized for maximum reflectivity at 275 nm (Table 1 and Fig. 3 captions). Measurements and simulations are at 30 deg.\nReported: Fig. 3 / Sec. 5 p. 185 (30 deg, unpolarized spectrophotometer, witness sample): reflectivity about 96 % at 275 nm; measurement agrees with the FilmStar simulation except below 250 nm (HfO2 absorption larger than modelled). From Fig. 3 (read off the plot, approximate): simulated R ~95-96 % from ~240 to ~320 nm, falling to ~78 % near 600 nm; measured curve rises steeply from ~50 % at ~205 nm. Fig. 4: mean measured reflectivity averaged over 250-350 nm at 30 deg for the first 32 production mirrors = 95.5 +/- 0.9 %. Fig. 5: double reflection reduces the HPD-weighted photon yield by ~15 % over 2-6 eV (<QE> = 0.176 vs <QE R^2> = 0.149). Sec. 1 goals: R > 94 % (250-300 nm) and > 80 % (210-500 nm).\nNotes: The paper builds the stack substrate-up (borosilicate glass 6 mm / Cr 20 nm adherence layer / Al 85 nm / SiO2 28 nm / HfO2 38 nm); listed here reversed, from the air side: HfO2 on top (Sec. 5: 'The hafnium oxide layer which terminates the stack'). The 85 nm Al film is optically opaque in 200-600 nm (transmission ~1e-4 or less), so Al is used as the semi-infinite substrate and the Cr layer and glass are omitted. Thicknesses are 'typical' geometric values from the quartz crystal monitor (Table 1), uniformity +/- 5 % (Sec. 3). No optical constants are printed (FilmStar library data was used; paper notes HfO2 k below 250 nm is underestimated), so the generic SiO2/HfO2 database dispersions and measured Al data are used; the UV results are sensitive to the Al and HfO2 data. Incidence medium air; Fig. 3 measured at 30 deg with an unpolarized spectrophotometer (Perkin Elmer Lambda 15), so compare RA (average of s and p). Two layers, total 66 nm (dielectric).\nLayers from Table 1 p. 185 (geometric thicknesses Cr 20, Al 85, SiO2 28, HfO2 38 nm); Sec. 5 p. 184 (stack Al + 1 pair SiO2/HfO2, HfO2 terminates the stack); Fig. 3; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R 97.0 % at 275 nm, mean 96.3 % over 250-350 nm, 30 deg (Rakic Al, generic oxides); paper R ~96 % at 275 nm; measured mean 95.5 +/- 0.9 %. The paper gives no optical constants; Rakic aluminium and the generic SiO2 and HfO2 are used.",
